In this code, `Array.newInstance()` is used to create a new array of the same component type as the original array, but with a size of 1 greater than the original array. The `System.arraycopy()` method is then used to copy the elements of the original array to the new array, starting from index 0 and including all elements up to the length of the original array. Finally, the new array is returned.

private static Object copyArrayGrow1(Object array,Class<?> newArrayComponentType) {
    if (array == null) {
        array = Array.newInstance(newArrayComponentType, 1);
    } else {
        Object[] newArray = (Object[]) Array.newInstance(array.getClass().getComponentType(), array.length + 1);
        System.arraycopy(array, 0, newArray, 0, array.length);
        array = newArray;
    }
    return array;
}

public Object copyArrayGrow1(Object array, Class<?> newArrayComponentType) {
    if (array == null) {
        array = Array.newInstance(newArrayComponentType, 1);
    } else {
        array = Arrays.copyOf(array, Array.getLength(array) + 1);
    }
    return array;
}

